g about the cost minimization condition. In the long run, the firm picks the optimal capital (K) to minimize costs. For each level of output, the firm chooses K such that the SRAC is minimized. But how is that expressed algebraically?\n\nWait, in the short run, the firm can't change capital, so the SRAC is given. In the long run, the firm can choose capital. The condition for cost minimization in the long run would involve choosing K such that the SRAC is minimized for that output level. But how does that translate into an equation?\n\nAlternatively, the condition that in the long run, the firm operates at the minimum point of the SRAC curve. But that might not be directly the case. Wait, no. In the long run, the firm can choose any capital level. So for each output level, the firm picks the capital that minimizes the average cost. Therefore, the long-run average cost is the minimum of all possible SRACs for that output. Therefore, the condition would be that the SRAC is equal to the LRAC, and the derivative of SRAC with respect to output is equal to the derivative of LRAC with respect to output. That is, the slopes are equal at the point of tangency.\n\nBut the question is about the condition that involves SRAC. So maybe the condition is that the SRAC is tangent to the LRAC. Which would mean that SRAC = LRAC and that their marginal costs are equal. But since in the long run, the firm can choose capital, the marginal cost in the long run (LRMC) equals the SRMC at the optimal capital level. Wait, but how does that relate to SRAC?\n\nAlternatively, the condition for the firm to be at the optimal capital level in the long run is that the short-run average cost is minimized. But that's in the short run. Wait, no. In the long run, the firm can adjust capital. So the firm will choose capital such that the SRAC is minimized for that output. But how is that expressed?\n\nLet me think about the relationship between the LRAC and SRAC. The LRAC is the lower envelope of the SRA