pital is chosen optimally.\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me try to approach it from another angle. In the long run, the firm chooses both labor and capital to minimize cost. The cost minimization condition is that the ratio of marginal products equals the ratio of input prices. That is, MP_L / MP_K = w / r, where w is the wage rate and r is the rental rate of capital. But how does this relate to SRAC?\n\nAlternatively, in the long run, the firm will choose the capital level such that the SRAC is minimized. So, for a given output, the firm picks K such that the SRAC is minimized. Therefore, the condition would be that the derivative of SRAC with respect to output is zero. But that's the same as the short-run condition. However, in the long run, the firm can adjust capital, so the SRAC is not fixed. Therefore, the firm can choose K to make the SRAC as low as possible. Therefore, the condition that the firm is at the optimal capital level in the long run is that the SRAC is minimized for that output level. Which would be when the derivative of SRAC with respect to output is zero. But that seems to be the same as the short-run condition. However, in the long run, the firm can adjust capital, so the SRAC is not fixed. Therefore, the firm can choose K to make the SRAC curve such that it is minimized. Therefore, the condition is that the SRAC is minimized, which is when the derivative of SRAC with respect to output is zero. But that seems to be the same as the short-run condition. However, in the long run, the firm can adjust capital, so the SRAC is not fixed. Therefore, the firm can choose K to make the SRAC curve such that it is minimized. Therefore, the condition is that the SRAC is minimized, which is when the derivative of SRAC with respect to output is zero. But that seems to be the same as the short-run condition. However, in the long run, the firm can adjust capital, so the SRAC is not fixed. Therefore, the firm can choose K to make the SRAC curve such that it is